      "whatExisted": "Two branches of work: exact attention that was too slow, and approximate attention that gave up exactness to be fast.",
      "problem": "Both branches assumed the cost was arithmetic. It was not - it was memory traffic, moving the attention matrix to and from high-bandwidth memory.",
      "mechanism": "Never materialise the full attention matrix. Tile the computation, keep tiles in fast on-chip memory, and use a running softmax so the result is exact without ever writing the intermediate.",
      "whatItFixed": "Attention got several times faster and used far less memory, with a bit-for-bit exact result - no approximation anywhere.",
      "newTradeoff": "The saving is in memory movement, not arithmetic, so the asymptotic cost is still quadratic; and the gain comes from hardware-specific kernels that must be rewritten for each new accelerator.",
      "buys": "A large constant-factor speedup and a big memory saving, while staying exact.",
      "givesUp": "Nothing mathematically - which is why it is on this list as the exception. What it costs is portability: it is a kernel, not an idea you can express in a few lines of framework code.",
